data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"researcher","content":"- CRISPR gene editing modifies genomes. It is based on a simplified version of the bacterial CRISPR-Cas9 antiviral defense system [1].\n- A synthetic guide RNA paired with the Cas9 nuclease is delivered into a cell, which cuts the genome at a desired location so genes can be removed or new ones added [1].\n- In prokaryotes, CRISPR sequences come from bacteriophage DNA and help detect and destroy DNA from similar phages in later infections [2].\n- Other gene-editing tools include zinc finger nucleases, TALENs, and meganucleases. Gene editing makes targeted insertions, deletions, or substitutions in DNA [3].\n- Hacker News links cite a 2021 story on CRISPR and treating diseases [4] and a 2024 report that HIV in cell culture was eliminated with CRISPR-Cas editing [5]. These sources give only headlines and metadata, so the details are unverified."}

